  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The <b>correct approach</b> is to terminate SSL at the load balancer, encrypt data stored on EBS via Amazon EBS encryption, and enable server-side encryption (SSE) for Amazon S3. This ensures data is protected both <b>in transit</b> (via SSL/TLS from client to load balancer) and <b>at rest</b> (with automatic encryption on persistent storage in EBS and S3).<br><br><b>Why the right answer is correct:</b><br>- SSL at the load balancer ensures traffic from clients is secured in transit.<br>- EBS encryption guarantees that any PHI stored on attached disks is always encrypted at rest.<br>- S3 SSE provides a robust mechanism for server-side object encryption in the object store.<br><br><b>Why the other answers are incorrect:</b><br>- <i>\"Set up SSL termination on the load balancer with a SAN SSL certificate, deploy EC2 instances with all EBS volumes encrypted, and utilize S3 with server-side encryption managed by customer keys.\"</i>: Using a SAN certificate is not required for PHI and adds unnecessary complexity. Customer-managed keys may introduce additional operational overhead without compliance necessity.<br><br>- <i>\"Configure the load balancer to use TCP load balancing, apply SSL termination on the EC2 instances, set up OS-level encryption on EBS volumes, and activate server-side encryption for S3 buckets.\"</i>: Terminating SSL on the EC2 instance increases potential attack surface and complexity; native EBS encryption is preferred over OS-level for guaranteed AWS-managed encryption.<br><br>- <i>\"Utilize TCP load balancing at the load balancer, set up SSL on EC2 instances, and apply server-side encryption for Amazon S3 data.\"</i>: This omits explicit EBS encryption direction, and terminating SSL at instances is not as efficient or secure as at the load balancer.<br><br>- <i>\"Implement SSL termination at both the load balancer and EC2 instances, encrypt EBS volumes holding sensitive data, and use server-side encryption for S3 objects.\"</i>: Running SSL termination twice is unnecessary; SSL should be terminated once, and additional complexity does not improve security per compliance requirements.<br><br><b>Tip</b>: Always use AWS-managed encryption services for regulated data, as they provide simpler, more auditable compliance.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You are building an application managing confidential health records.<br>Compliance requirements demand encrypting all such data both during transit and while at rest.<br>The system leverages a three-layer architecture: information passes through a load balancer, gets processed and stored on Amazon EBS volumes, and finally is saved on Amazon S3 using the AWS SDK.</p><p>Which of the following approaches best accomplishes these encryption requirements?</p>",
